Chapter: IdmSaltIvDistinct

  cites Kernel chapter IdentityManager
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The salt and the IV wrapping the identity private key were the SAME
 sixteen bytes, and constant for the life of the device: the generator
 peeked a fixed address with no state, so every call answered alike and a
 passphrase change re-derived the pair it already had. Nothing executed
 that path, which is why it stood. These three lines are what a reader of
 `idm-derive-random` cannot get from its body: that its two call sites
 must disagree, and that the same device at a later moment must disagree
 with itself.

Section: Body

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "T" else "F"

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let salt-a = idm-derive-random 1 1000 16
   in let iv-a = idm-derive-random 2 1000 16
   in let salt-b = idm-derive-random 1 2000 16
   in act
    print-line-uni ("salt-differs-from-iv=" & yn (idm-bytes-eq salt-a iv-a == False))
    print-line-uni ("salt-differs-across-time=" & yn (idm-bytes-eq salt-a salt-b == False))
    print-line-uni ("salt-is-deterministic=" & yn (idm-bytes-eq salt-a (idm-derive-random 1 1000 16)))
    print-line-uni ("width=" & show (list-length salt-a))
   end
  end

